\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Logical resources of the three filters on the shared events, in the common currency of controlled-$e^{\pm iAt}$ calls (all three use the same synthesised evolution circuit; one call costs $6.2\times 10^{5}$ CX at $T=400$ globally, $\approx 900$ CX on the largest 16-segment cluster). The scaling column gives the $T$-dependence of each row; anchors are quoted at the stated $T$. The HHL column is a structure-level estimate at $n_c=6$--$8$ clock bits; the 1BQF and comb columns are measured. In the per-cluster route only the \emph  {number} of cluster instances grows with $T$ (linearly); every per-instance resource is set by the cluster size, which the no-giant-component measurement caps at $\approx 17$ segments. One-qubit gate counts track the CX counts at the same order and are omitted.\relax }}{27}{table.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:resources}{{2}{27}{Logical resources of the three filters on the shared events, in the common currency of controlled-$e^{\pm iAt}$ calls (all three use the same synthesised evolution circuit; one call costs $6.2\times 10^{5}$ CX at $T=400$ globally, $\approx 900$ CX on the largest 16-segment cluster). The scaling column gives the $T$-dependence of each row; anchors are quoted at the stated $T$. The HHL column is a structure-level estimate at $n_c=6$--$8$ clock bits; the 1BQF and comb columns are measured. In the per-cluster route only the \emph {number} of cluster instances grows with $T$ (linearly); every per-instance resource is set by the cluster size, which the no-giant-component measurement caps at $\approx 17$ segments. One-qubit gate counts track the CX counts at the same order and are omitted.\relax }{table.caption.17}{}}
\citation{fowler2012}
\citation{camps2023}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces The rows of table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:resources} against track count. (a) Logical register width: all constructions follow $\lceil \qopname  \relax o{log}_2 4T^2\rceil $ plus a constant: the 1BQF and DSS--QETU comb lines coincide exactly (the comb pays no width for its degree on this primitive), while the dense-dilation LCU pays $+5$ and the HHL clock band $+7$ to $+9$. (b) Measured two-qubit gate cost per shot: the shared evolution call grows as $\approx T^{1.4}$ (pair count $\times $ ladder length), the three algorithms sitting at fixed multiples of it (2, 40, $\approx 130$--$510$ calls). The green diamonds are the largest single per-cluster instance at each $T$: set by the local cluster size, not by $T$, the flat exception to every other curve's growth.\relax }}{28}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rescmp}{{15}{28}{The rows of table~\ref {tab:resources} against track count. (a) Logical register width: all constructions follow $\lceil \log _2 4T^2\rceil $ plus a constant: the 1BQF and DSS--QETU comb lines coincide exactly (the comb pays no width for its degree on this primitive), while the dense-dilation LCU pays $+5$ and the HHL clock band $+7$ to $+9$. (b) Measured two-qubit gate cost per shot: the shared evolution call grows as $\approx T^{1.4}$ (pair count $\times $ ladder length), the three algorithms sitting at fixed multiples of it (2, 40, $\approx 130$--$510$ calls). The green diamonds are the largest single per-cluster instance at each $T$: set by the local cluster size, not by $T$, the flat exception to every other curve's growth.\relax }{figure.caption.18}{}}
